          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux often happens after meals because a full stomach puts more pressure on the valve at its top, and that valve sometimes lets stomach contents back up into the food pipe (esophagus). The main mechanism is brief relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter, the ring of muscle at the junction between esophagus and stomach (source: *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*).\n\nThe retrieved sources explain these factors:\n\n- **Meal size:** A large meal stretches the stomach and raises pressure against the valve. The same food split into smaller portions usually causes fewer symptoms (source: *Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers*).\n- **Fat content:** Fatty meals empty from the stomach more slowly. This keeps stomach contents there longer, which extends the time reflux can happen. The effect depends partly on how long the meal stays, so a heavy late dinner often causes more trouble than the same food at midday (same source).\n- **Posture:** Lying down removes gravity's help in keeping stomach contents down. This is why a full stomach at bedtime is a common problem (same source). Symptoms also tend to worsen on bending forward (source: *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*).\n\nThe sources do not explain in detail why the sphincter relaxes after eating, so I can't go further on that point. They also do not describe the hormonal or nerve signals involved.\n\nA few things can make reflux worse, including alcohol, carbonated drinks, smoking, excess body weight, and some medicines that relax the sphincter (source: *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*). Do not change any medicine on your own. Ask the prescriber first.\n\nIf reflux happens often, or if you have symptoms such as trouble swallowing, weight loss, or vomiting blood, see a clinician promptly. Seek urgent care right away for severe chest pain, especially with breathlessness, sweating, or pain spreading to the arm or jaw, since these can signal a heart problem.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
